The Committee of the Assembly, which is currently drafting legislation on the proclamation of the name change, stated: "Of course the final decision on the namining members of the Members of the Assembly will depend on the members themselves."
Welsh Government's 2017 law gave the Welsh assembly the tag to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a consultation with the public, and the latter’s proposal for naming the assembly as “Welsh Parliament“ found widespread support.
With regard to the title issue of the Assembly’s Members, the Commission favored the designation “Members of the Parliament of Wales“ or “WMP“, but the “MWP“ alternative found more support in a public consultation.
Apparently, Assembly members are suggesting alternative elections, but the fight to reach a consensus could turn into headaches for Chairman Elin Jones, from which legislation on changes is expected in a few weeks.
Legislation on reforms will include other changes related to the way the assembly operates, including rules for the exclusion of Assembly Members and the structure of the committee system.
During the discussion of the legislation, the final vote regarding the issue of their namination will remain to the Members of the Assembly.
Macedonians vote in the referendum on changing the country's name.
On Sunday, voters will vote to change their country’s name to “Republic of North Macedonia”.”
Voting by the people was decided in an effort to resolve a long-standing dispute with neighboring Greece, which also has a province called Macedonia.
Athens has long insisted that its northern neighbor's name constitutes a claim over its territory and has repeatedly opposed its demands for EU and NATO membership.
Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of the name change referendum, has stated that he will not recognize the votes.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that changing the name is simply the price to be paid for EU and NATO membership.
