Sainsbury plans entry into UK cosmetics
Sainsbury's is complecting Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams with large store-style cosmetic pavilions, with a specialist vending staff.
In the framework of a significant effort to enter the €£2.8-billar UK cosmetic market, which is continuing to grow as sales of fashion clothing and home furnishings decline, the largest cosmetic pavilions will be tested in 11 stores around the country and will spread to more stores the following year if they are successful.
The investment in cosmetics comes at a time when supermarkets are looking for ways to fill shelves spaces that were once used for televisions, microwaves and home furnishings.
Sainsbury’s has stated that it would double its offer of cosmetic products up to 3000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
Even the existing lines from L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees will be given more space, with areas dedicated to the respective brands, similar to those located in stores like Boots.
Also, the supermarket will roll out its boutique cosmetic line again, so that most of the products are vegan - something that is increasingly being demanded by buyers of younger ages.
In addition, the “Fragrance Shop”’s retailor will test concessions at two Sainsbury’’’s shops, the first of which was opened last week in Croydon, south of London, while a second one is expected to open later this year in Sally Oak, Birmingham.
Online shopping and a tendency towards the daily purchase of foods in small quantities in neighborhood stores mean supermarkets now need to do more to attract people.
Mike Coupe, Sainsbury��’s chief executive, has said outlets will begin to look more and morely with large stores, as the supermarket network tries to fight Aldi’s and Lidl’s price cuts with more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury’s outlets has deployed “Argos” in hundreds of stores and has introduced a series of “Habitat” since the acquisition of both networks two years ago, which according to him has boosted sales of food products and maked purchases more profitable.
The supermarket’s previous attempt to restructure its cosmetic and pharmaceutical departments resulted in failure.
Sainsbury's tested a company merger with Boots in the early 2000s, but the merger dissolved after a debate over how to share pharmacies in his supermarkets.
The new strategy comes three years after Sainsbury’s selled his 281-store business to Celesio’’s pharmaceutical, the owner of the “Loyds” pharmaceutical network for “125 million pounds.”
“Lloyds” would play a role in the plan, adding an enhanced range of luxury skincare brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy in four stores.
“Paul Mills-Hicks, Sainsbury’’s’ commercial executive, said: “We’ve transformed the look of our cosmetic pavilions to improve the environment in favor of our customers.
We have also invested in a staff with specialized training, which will be willing to give advice.
The range of our brands is designed in such a way as it suits every need, and the attractive environment and convenient locations show that we are now a glamorous cosmetic destination, which makes a difference from the old way of making purchases.”
