More than 20 million people attended Brett Kavanaugh's court hearing
Over 20 million people have followed through six television networks the interesting testimony of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh and the woman who accused him of a sexual assault suspected to have taken place in the 1980s, Christine Blasey Ford.
Meanwhile, the political deadlock continued, and broadcasters interrupted regular programs to air the last-minute turn of Friday's events: an agreement reached by Arizona Sen. Jeff Flake for conducting a week-long investigation by the FBI over the allegations.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee that it is 100 percent certain that Kavanaugh had touched him while he was drunk and had tried to unveil him at a gymnasium.
Kavanaugh, in an ardent testimony, said it was 100 per cent sure this hadn't happened.
It is likely that the number of followers was greater than that of 20.4 million people, reported by Nielsen on Friday.
The company was measuring average visibility on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News and MSNBC.
Figures for other streaming networks, including PBS, C-SPAN and the Fox Business network, weren't immediately available.
And Nielsen usually has problems counting viewers from the offices.
To put this figure in perspective, the size of this audience is similar to that of a football eliminator or that of the Academy Awards.
The Fox News channel, whose opinionists have strongly supported Kavanaugh's nomination, topped other networks with an average of 5.69 million viewers during the 24-hough session, Nielsen said.
ABC came in second place with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million, Nielsen said.
interest continued to remain high even after the end of the court hearing.
Flake was the main character in Friday's drama.
