“I’m standing here, right in front of you,” a woman said.
"Do you think he's telling the country the truth?
“He said, ‘You have the power, while there are so many helpless women.��’”
Flake said his office had issued a statement, and before the elevator door closed, he said there would be more to say during the committee's hearing.
Hours later, all cable and satellite networks were directly covering the moment the Judiciary Committee was due to vote for Kavanaugh's candidacy to vote in front of all members of the Senate.
But Flake said he would only do so with the conviction that the FBI would investigate the allegations against the candidate over the next week, as the Democratic minority had requested.
Flake had been partially persuaded by conversations with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After a conversation with Coons and several other senators behind him, Flake made the decision.
Flake's choice was smart, as it was clear that Republicans wouldn't have enough votes to approve Kavanaugh without conducting the investigation.
