Uragan Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas, Salt Lake City with immediate flooding (which could pose an advantage for drought zones)
Although it rarely happens that a tropical depression hit Arizona, this is probably exactly what will happen in the early days of next week, as Hurricane Rosa's residual power passes through the Southwest desert, spreading the risk of immediate flooding.
The National Meteorological Service has already issued warnings of immediate flooding for Monday and Tuesday from western Arizona to southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to take the helm to Phoenix on Tuesday, approaching in the late hours of Monday with raining.
The Phoenix National Meteorological Service said in a tweet that "ten tropical cyclones have received the status of storm or tropical depression in a 200-mile radius from Phoenix since 1950!
Katrina (1967) was a 40-mile hurricane from the Arizona border."
The latest models of the National Hurricane Center predict 2 to 4-inches of precipitation, with insulated amounts of up to 6 inches at Arizona’’’s Mogollon border.
In other areas of the Southwest, including Central Rockies and the Greater Basin, rainfall is expected to be 1 to 2-inch levels, while in isolated areas their total could reach 4 inches.
For out-of-the-risk areas of immediate flooding, Rosa's rainfall could be a blessing, as the region has been battered by drought.
Although the flooding poses a serious concern, some of these rainfalls may be beneficial, as the Southwest is currently grappling with drought conditions.
“Under the U.S. Drought Monitoring Center, just over 40 percent of Arizona is facing extreme second-tier drought,” weather.com reported.
Initially, Hurricane Rosa will hit Mexico’s Baja California peninsula.
Rosa, still with hurricane power on Sunday morning, with winds at top speeds of 85 miles per hour, is located 385 miles south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico, and is moving north north at speeds of 12 miles per hour.
The storm is grappling with cold water over the Pacific, and it's losing strength.
Consequently, it is expected that the hurricane will hit Mexico with tropical storm powers in the afternoon or on Monday evening.
Rairfall over some areas of Mexico could be severe, posing significant risk of flooding.
�“Rainy rainfall of an average level of 3 to 6 inches is expected from Baja California to the northwestern Sonora, and can reach up to 10 inches“, weather.com reported.
Rosa will then move north through Mexico in the form of a tropical storm, before reaching the border with Arizona in the early hours of Tuesday morning as tropical depression, which will then move through Arizona, reaching southern Utah on Tuesday night.
“The main danger expected from Rosa or its consequences are the strong rainfall in Baja California, in the northwestern Sonora and in the southwestern U.S. desert”, the National Hurricane Center said.
These rainfalls are expected to cause immediate life-threatening flooding, debris leaks in deserts and landslides on mountainous terrain.
