Hundred killed in Indonesia's earthquake and tsunami, as figures are expected to rise
At least 384 people have been killed, many of whom were devoured by giant waves that collided on beaches when Indonesia's Sulawesi island was hit by an earthquake and tsunami, authorities said on Saturday.
Hundred people had gathered for a beach festival in the city of Palu on Friday wheels of up to six-meter (18-feets) slammed to shore at twilight, engulfing and causing the deaths of many people and destroying everything came forward.
The tsunami followed a 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
“Yesterday, when the tsunami threat arose, people were still carrying out their usual activities on the beach, and therefore did not run immediately and fell victim to him,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, a spokesman for the Indonesian Disaster Prevention Agency, told a “press conference.”
“The tsunami didn’t come alone, it took with him cars, trunks, houses, hit everything above the ground”, Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami had traversed the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h (497 miles per hour), before hitting the coastline.
Some people climbed the trees to escape the tsunami and survived, he said.
About 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centres in Peru.
Aerial photographs published by the disaster agency showed a range of destroyed buildings and shops, twisted and collapsed bridges and a windshield surrounded by water.
Tremors after the quake continued to rock the coastal city on Saturday.
Earthquakes was felt in an area of 2.4 million people.
The Indonesian Agency for Assessmentation and Application of Technology (BPPT) said in a statement that the energy emitted by Friday's massive earthquake was about triple the power of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima during World War II.
The geographical position of the city, which is located at the edge of a long and narrow bay, may have multiplied the power of the tsunami, he said.
Nugroho described the damage as "extremely great" and said thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
Several corpses were found trapped under the rubble of collapsed buildings, he said, adding that 540 people had been injured and 29 had disappeared.
Nugroho said the death toll and the extent of the damage could be much greater along the 300 km (100-mile) coastline north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, which is closest to the epicenter of the earthquake.
The communications networks "were completely interrupted without any warning" from Donggala, Nugroho said.
That area has over 300,000 residents", the Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its staff and volunteers were traveling to the affected areas.
“This has already turned into a tragedy, but the situation could even worse,” he said.
On Saturday, the agency was harshly criticized for noticing Palu's crackdown by the tsunami, despite officials saying the waves had come at when the warning was given.
In amateur footage shared on social media, a man upstairs of a building can be heard warning passerbers on the way down about the arrival of the tsunam with furious screams.
Within a few minutes, a wave slammed on the shore, taking with him buildings and cars.
Reuters failed to immediately verify the authenticity of the footage.
Earthquake and tsunami triggered a massive power outage, which cut communications across Palu, making it difficult to coordinate rescue efforts by the authorities.
The military has begun sending cargo planes with aid from Jakarta and other cities, authorities said, but evacuated people continue to have great needs for food and other basic needs.
The city's airport has only reopened for rescue efforts and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo was expected to visit evacuation centres in Palu on Sunday.
The death toll from the tsunami in Indonesia goes above 800.
The situation is severe enough.
