U.S. needs to double funding for artificial intelligence
As China becomes increasingly active in artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double the amount spent on research in this area, says investor and artificial intelligence expert Kai-Fu Lee, who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments have come after various parts of the U.S. government have announced artificial intelligence revelations, despite the U.S. lacks an official overall strategy for artificial intelligence.
Meanwhile, China has announced its plan last year: by 2030, it aims to take the top spot for innovation in the field of artificial intelligence.
“Doubling the budget for artificial intelligence research would be a good start, given that other countries are still far behind the U.S. and we are looking for the next big discovery in this area,” Lee said.
Doubling the funding could double the chances that the next major achievement in the field of artificial intelligence will take place in the U.S., Lee said in an interview with CNBC this week.
Leé, author of the book titled "The Superpowers of Artificial Intelligence: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order", which was released this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the executive director of Sinovation Ventures, who has invested in one of China's most prominent artificial intelligence companies, Face++.
In the 1980s, at “Carnegie Mellon” university, he created an artificial intelligence system that he also transferred to the American player in Othello, and then worked as chief executive at Microsoft Research and as president of Google’s China branch.
Lee congratulated previous U.S. government competitions for technology, such as the Agency's Robotics Challenge for Advanced Defense Research Projects, and asked when the next contest would take place, in order to help identify new visionaries.
Leé said that often, U.S. researchers have to work hard to receive grants from the government.
““It’s not China that’s removing academic leaders; it’s the corporations,” Lee said.
Over the past few years, Facebook, Google and other tech companies have hired bright minds from universities to work in the field of artificial intelligence.
Lee said changes in immigration policy could help the U.S. impetus its efforts in the field of artificial intelligence. “
“I think that green cards should automatically be given to people with the title of a doctor in the field of artificial intelligence,”” he said.
In July 2017, the State Council of Chane implemented the Plan for the Development of the New Generation of Artificial Intelligence.
China's National Foundation for Natural Sciences provides funding to people in academic institutions, just as the National Science Foundation and other government organizations give money to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work is lower in China, Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense set up a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, which is expected to include partners from industry and academia, and the White House announced the establishment of the Selection Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
Meanwhile, this month DARPA announced a $2 billion-dollar investment at an initiative called “AI Next.” “AI.”
In terms of NSF, it currently invests over $100 million a year for artificial intelligence research.
Meanwhile, U.S. legislation aimed to create a National Artificial Intelligence Safety Commission has not been implemented for months.
Macedonians vote in referendum on changing the country's name
On Sunday, the people of Macedonia voted in a referendum to decide whether its name will be changed to the �“Republic of North Macedonia”, a move which could resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece, which had blocked its efforts for membership in the European Union and NATO.”
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, thinks its northern neighbor's name constitutes a claim over its territory, and has vetoed its blocking NATO and EU membership.
The two governments reached an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change could compromise the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic majority.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said that he will not vote in the referendum, and a boycott campaign has cast doubt on whether turnout in the elections will reach 50 percent necessary for the validity of the referendum.
The question on the referendum ballot reads: "Are you in favor of NATO and EU membership through the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?"
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that for Macedonia, as one of the countries created by the dissolution of Yugoslavia, this is a price worth paying to achieve membership in structures such as the EU and NATO.
"I have come today to vote for the future of the country, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live free under the umbrella of the European Union, as this means a safer life for all of us", said 79-year-old Olivera Georgijevska in Skopje.
Although the referendum has no binding power, a sufficient number of members of parliament have said they will stick to the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
The name change requires a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The State Election Commission said that no irregularities had been reported until 1 p.m.
However, turnout turnout was just 16 per cent, compared to 34 per cent in the recent parliamentary elections of 2016, where 66 percent of registered voters cast their vote.
““I came to vote for the sake of my children, we have the place in Europe”,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62 years older in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia’s Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dusko cast their vote in the referendum on Macedonia’s name change, which would pave the way for the country’s membership in NATO and the European Union, in Strumica, Macedonia, 30 September 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkow was preparing a small scene and setting a chair in front of tents set up by referendum boycotters.
"We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to join our heads held high, and not by entering through the back door," Kavadarkov said.
“Our country is poor, but we have dignity.”
“If they don’t like us named Macedonia, we can turn to other countries like China and Russia and become part of Euro-Asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev says that NATO membership will bring much-needed investments for Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of over 20 percent.
“I think that the vast majority will be in favor, as more than 80 percent of our citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO,” Zaev said after throwing his vote.
He expressed that a positive result would "confirm our future".
According to a poll published last Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Political Research, about 30 to 43 percent of voters would participate in the referendum - a figure below the necessary level of turnout.
Another poll conducted by Macedonian Telma television found that 57 per cent of participants planned to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 percent stated that they would vote in favor.
For a successful referendum, the turnout rate should be 50 per cent plus one vote.
“The failure of the referendum would give the first strong blow to the pro-Western government’s policy since it came to power in May last year.
