He referred by name to the Democratic Chairman of the Senate Judiciary Committee, Dianne Feinstein, which sparked cheers of discontent from the audience.
Don't you remember her response?
Did you spread the document?
Well, what?
No, huh, no, I... wait a little... - her body language was bad enough - it was the worst body language I've ever seen."
The Labor Party is no longer an �“open church.”
She's non-tolerant to those who say what they think
When Momentum activists in my local party voted in favor of the no-confidence motion against me, I wasn’t so surprised.
Ultimately, it's not me the first of the Labour Party MPs who are told they're not welcome - all this because we say what we think.
My parliamentarian colleague Joan Ryan was treated similarly, simply because he resolutely opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, the no-confidence motion was a critique of disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
Regarding the importance of a responsible economic policy, related to national security and with Europe, ironically similar issues for which Jeremy contradicted previous leaders.
In the announcement of the Labor Party meeting in Nottingham East, it was stated that “we want the meetings to be inclusive and productive.” ”
For most of my eight-year period as a local deputy of the Labor Party, GC meetings have been just like this.
“Unfortunately, meetings no longer have their previous tone, and the promise of a “softer and kinder” policy has been forgotten for a lone, if it ever existed.
It has become increasingly apparent that opposing views are not tolerated in the Labour Party, and every opinion is judged on the basis of its eligibility by the party's leadership.
This began a while after Jeremy became a leader, while colleagues with whom I thought I shared similar political views started pretending that I would take a 180-degree turnaround and take positions with which I would never have agreed - either on national security or the EU's single market.
Whenever I speak in public — no matter what I say — criticism on social media breaks out, with people demanding my dismissal, they denounce the center’s policies tell me they shouldn’’’’’t be part of the Labor Party.
I didn’t just suffer this.
I'm aware that I'm luckier than some of my colleagues, as comments about me tend to be of a political nature.
I am fascinated by the professionalism and determination of colleagues facing gender and racial discrimination every day, but they don’t.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political era is the normalization of levels of violence.
Last week, Jeremy Corbin said that the Labor Party should nurture a culture of tolerance.
In reality, we are no longer an “open church”, and with every motion of confidence and with any change of selection rules, the party becomes increasingly closed.
Over the last couple of years, I’ve often been advised to lower my head, not to be so noisy, and that thus everything “will “it’s going well.”
But that's not why I've entered politics.
Since I joined the Labor Party 32 years ago as a school student, fueled by the disregard of Thatcher's government that led my class to failure at a general school, I've tried to lobbie for better public services for those most in need - either as a member of a local council or government minister.
I've never concealed my politics, not even in the last election.
Everyone in Nottingham East had clear my political stances and points of opposition to current leadership.
For all those who voted in favor of the motion on Friday, I only want to say that the country is heading towards a Brexit that will affect our families, businesses and public services. I do not understand the desire to waste time and energy with my loyalty to the leader of the Labor Party.
But indeed, the only message I want to convey is not directed to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, whether or not members of the Labor Party: I feel proud to be at your service and I promise you that no threat of dismissal or political favor will prevent me from acting in your best interest.
Chris Leslie is Nottingham East MP
