�“The sea is ours”: Bolivia unexploded at sea hopes court will pave the way to the Pacific
Sailors patrol an improfision of the navy's headquarters in La Paz.
Public buildings have erected the blue ocean flag.
On naval bases from Lake Titicaca in the Amazon is marked the motto: "The sea is our property.
Reclaiming him is a task."
Across Bolivina, the memory of Chile's takeover of the coast in a bloody conflict for resources during the 19th century is still quite alive - as does the desire to sail again across the Pacific Ocean.
Now, those hopes are more likely than they've ever been for decades, with Bolivia waiting for the International Court of Justice's decision after a five-year review.
“Bolivia is characterized by impetus, the spirit of unity and tranquility, and of course, is waiting with positivity for the result”, said Roberto Calzadella, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will attend the ICJ’s session on large screens across the country, in the hope that the Hague tribunal will decide in Bolivia’s — after decades of irregular discussions — Chile is obliged to hold talks to give Bolivia a sovereign seaway territory.
For Evo Morales, Bolivia's indigenous indignant president - who is facing a controversial battle to get a second term in next year's election - Monday's decision is quite important.
“We are very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean”, he pledged at the end of August.
But some analysts are of the opinion that it is very unlikely that the court will rule in Bolivia's favor - and that even if little would change.
The Dutch-based U.N. body does not have the right to give another country from Chile’s territory, and has said it will not decide the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the ICJ is coming up with a decision just six months after hearing the final arguments shows that the issue "was not complicated", said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert of international law.
And instead of helping Bolivia's cause, the events of the last four years may have penalised it.
"The issue of access to the sea has been compromised by the current Bolivian administration", said Zárate.
Morale's aggressive rhetoric has eradicated any trace of benevolence left in the Chileans, she suggested.
“At a certain point, Bolivia and Chile’ will continue talks, but it’s going to be extremely difficult for discussions to take place.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, objected to the idea that the court's decision-making was surprisingly swift.
Monday will offer Bolivia “an extraordinary opportunity to open a new era of relations with Chile” and “an “ending 139-year-year-old disagreements,” he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales — still one of Latin America’s most popular presidents — was using the maritime issue for political interests.
"Bolivia will never give up her right to have access to the Pacific Ocean," he added.
“Decision is an opportunity to realize that we have to leave the past behind.”
