John Edward: Language capabilities essential for global citizens
Private schools in Scotland hold the record for academic excellence and this has also continued in 2018 with another queue of excellent results in exams, further reinforced by individual and collective success in sports, art, music and other fields.
With over 30,000 pupils across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Private Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the highest level of service to pupils and their parents.
Private schools aim to prepare their pupils for further education of the highest levels, for their chosen careers and their country as global citizens.
As an educational sector that can design and implement an adapted educational curriculum, we are witnessing to how the branch of modern languages continues to be a widespread and well-liked choice in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, 'If you speak to someone in a language that he understands, your words come to him.
If you speak to him in his own language, your words come to his heart."
These words are a powerful reminder that we can't just rely on English when we want to establish relationships with people from foreign countries.
From the results of the last exams of this year, we can ascertain that the languages are leading the lists of results, with the highest rate of passing grades among private schools.
A total of 68 percent of students who have studied foreign languages have received the maximum grade A.
The data collected from 74 SCIS member schools showed that 72 percent of the students had received the maximum grade A in Mandarin, while 72 percent of those who studied German, 69 percent of those who studied for French and 63 percent of those who studied Spanish had also received grade A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland are supporting foreign languages as one of the vital skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Currently, the branch of foreign languages is considered just as important as STEM branches (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in private school curricula.
A 2014 study by the U.K. Employment and Skills Commission found that among the main difficulties faced in filling vacancies, 17 per cent of them were associated with a lack of language skills.
As a result, language skills are becoming increasingly indispensable for preparing young people for their future careers.
With the addition of jobs that have as a condition the mastery of foreign languages, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the career that a person chooses, if he has learned a second language, he will have real advantages in the future, as the owner of such long-term ability.
The ability to communicate directly with foreign people will automatically position a multilingual person on competitors.
According to a YouGov survey, which included over 4000 adults from the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 percent of them were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to conduct a conversation, and French was the only foreign language spoken by a percentage of 2-figure people, exactly 15 per cent.
That's why investing in language learning is important for today's children.
Possession of many languages, and especially the mastery of developing countries’ languages, will give children a better chance of employment.
Within Scotland, each school will be different in terms of the languages that are taught in them.
Some schools will focus on more classical modern languages, while others will offer languages that are considered the most important for the UK in the perspective of 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child’s interest, there will always be a range of languages from which you can choose in private schools, with pedagogical staff consisting of specialists in the field.
Kosovo’s private schools are committed to providing a school environment that will prepare children and “arrange” them with the skills needed to succeed, however.
In these times there is no denying the fact that in a global business environment, languages continue to have a vital importance for the future of the country, so this should be reflected in education.
Modern languages should indeed be regarded as “international communication skills”.”
Private schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to Scotland’s youth.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Head of the Scottish Council of Private Schools
