Europe leads 10-6 as it heads towards Ryder Cup final day
Europe has a substantial advantage in the final day of the Ryder Cup after coming out of four games and with pairs with a 10-6 advantage over the United States.
Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari topped the two-win against a hard-to-fit Tiger Woods to increase their advantage to four points at the ‘Le Golf National’.
Thomas Bjorn's European team, offered to take the trophy he lost in Hazeltine two years ago, dominated the helpless U.S. team team in the mid-door matches, bringing the results series to 3-1.
The U.S. team showed more resistance in the four-game, winning two games, but they couldn't reduce the difference.
Jim Furyk's team needs eight points from Sunday's 12 individual games to keep the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first start-to-early player to have won four points in a row, while he and Molinari, nicknamed ‘Molliwood’, after a sensational weekend are the second pair in the Ryder Cup history to earn four points in their opening four matches.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed into the foursome game, they combined well enough together to beat a helpless Woods and American beginner Bryson Dechambeau with an even more dramatic 5&4 score.
Woods, who held the two meetings on Saturday, displayed occasional outbursts of brilliance, but he has already lost 19 of his 29 games in the four-game game and with pairs and 7 consecutive games.
Justin Rose, rested for the four-morning match, returned to partner Henrik Stenson in the pair-to-play game and picked up a 2&1 defeat by Dustin Justin and Brooks Koepka - ranked first and third in the world.
Europe didn’t have everything in favor on a fairly beautiful and fresh day in the southwest of Paris.
The three-time professional winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas decided the difference that had to be picked up by the Americans by two points on Saturday.
They picked up a bold 2&1 victory against Spaniard Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter in the four-game matches and returned later to beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy 4&3 in pair matches after having lost in the first two potholes.
Only twice in the Rhyder Cup history a team has turned the score after a four-point difference in individual games, even though Furyk's holding team needs only a draw to keep the trophy.
However, having been the second-best player for two days, a counter-attack on Sunday looks set to go beyond their reach.
North Korea says it won’t disarmament ‘in any way’ unilaterally without gaining trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. that continued sanctions on Saturday were deepening mistrie over the U.S. and the country would not give up its nuclear weapons unilaterally in such circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho told the annual body from around the General Assembly that North Korea has undertaken "important measures that show goodwill" in the past year, such as the prohibition of nuclear and missile tests, dismantling areas where nuclear tests took place and pledging that it will not increase the arsenal of weapons and nuclear technology.
"However, we'ren't seeing any response regarding this from the U.S," he said.
"Without having confidence in the U.S., our national security won't have self-certainty, and in these circumstances there's no way we unilaterally disarm ourselves first."
While Reiterating North Korea��’s usual grievances regarding Washington’s resistance of a divided “in-stage” approach to the nuclear disarmament process where North Korea would be evaluated due to the gradual steps it has taken, its statement seemed important because it didn’t rule out unilateral nuclear disarmament, as Pyongyang has previously done.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at a first-time summit between a sitting U.S. president and the North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, where Kim pledged to work towards the "nuclear disarmament of the Korean peninsula" as Trump pledged assurances about North Korea's security.
North Korea has demanded an official end to the 1950-53 Korean War, but the United States has said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted calls to allevate strict international sanctions on North Korea.
"The U.S. insists that 'nuclear disarmament’ be carried out initially and increases the level of pressure through sanctions to achieve their goal bindingly, even by "descriminating the end of the war," Ri said.
“Percession that sanctions can bring us to our knees are dreams in the sun of people who are uninformed about us.”
But the problem is that the continuation of sanctions is deepening our mistrust."
Ri did not mention the plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, a statement that the U.S. leader stressed earlier this week at the United Nations.
Instead, the minister underlined three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in in the past five months and added: "If the party on the issue of nuclear disarmament was South Korea and not the U.S., the process of nuclear disarmament on the Korean peninsula would not have come in this situation with no way out."
Despite this, the tone used in the New speech was profoundly different from last year, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that the deployment of U.S. territory targeting North Korean missiles was inevitable after "Mr. Devil" Trump called Kim "man rocket" on a suicide mission.
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year threatened to "fully destroy" North Korea thanked Kim for the courage shown in taking steps for disarmament, but said there is still much work to be done and that sanctions must continue to be active until North Korea carries out nuclear disarmament.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn’t have a time frame for that, saying, “Whether it’s going to take two years, three years or five months — that doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: "Implementing Security Council sanctions must continue vigorously and non-stopping until we carry out the full and final verification of nuclear disarmament."
The Security Council has unanimously imposed increased sanctions on North Korea since 2006, in an effort to end funding Pyongyang’s nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri aside at the U.N. General Assembly and later said that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare a second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times already this year, but his last visit didn’t go so well.
He left Pyongyang in July saying there has been progress, and only within a few hours North Korea denounced it had carried out "mafia-style requirements."
North Korea pledged at a meeting with Moon this month to disarm a missile zone and also a nuclear complex if the United States undertake "relevant measures."
He said Kim had told him that the "relevant measures" he sought were security assurances that Trump pledged in Singapore and moves towards normalizing relations with Washington.
Harval students take lessons on how to get enough rest
“A new course at Harvard University this year’s” has involved all students to sleep more, in an effort to combat the study’’’’’’’’.
An academic noted that the world's number one university students often don’t know when it comes to the most basic things about how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and the Women's Hospital, prepared the lecture course, which he believes to be the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired to start this course after giving a presentation on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
“At the end of him, a girl approached me and said, ‘Whigh this thing I’m being told now, in my last year?’”
‘She said no one had spoken to her before about the importance of sleep – which surprised me,’ he told �‘The Telegraph’.
The course, included in the program for the first time this year, explains to students the necessary elements of how the habits of a good sleep help academic and athletic performance, and also how it improves their well-being in general.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and director general of the university’s medical services, said the university decided to introduce this course after finding that students were seriously deprived of sleep throughout the week.
The course that lasts an hour involves a variety of interactive tasks.
On the one hange is a view of a dormitory room, where students click on coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books so that they can be explained the effects of caffeine and lighting and how the athletic performance is affected by lack of sleep, as well as the importance of the routine of the hours to be spent in bed.
In another part, participants are explained how sleep deprivation in the long term increases the risks of heart attack, ischemia, depression and cancer.
A map of the camp, with interactive icons, further encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
"We know that students' behaviour won't change immediately.
But we believe that they have the right to know - as you have the right to know, the health impact when you choose to smoke," added Prof Czeisler.
The culture of pride to spend ‘a night of sleeplessness’ continues to exist, he said, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students meant that sleep deprivation was an ever-increasing problem.
Assuring that you’ve received enough dose of sleep, of good quality, should be students��’ ‘secret’ to combat stress, fatigue and anxiety, he said — even to avoid weight gain, because sleep deprivation takes the brain to a hunger mode, making them feel constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old califorman who studies physical and chemical biology, helped Professor Czeisler in preparing the course, having attended one of his hours of instruction last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and had inspired him to turn him into a course for the entire camp.
“He hopes that the next step is to ask all postgraduate students to complete a similar study program before joining competing institutions.
Prof Czeisler recommended that students should set a bell clock on when to go to bed, also when they need to wake up, and be aware of the damaging effects of the ‘blue ‘blue light’ coming from electronic screens and LED lighting, which can put the biological rhythm of sleep out of work, which leads to problems in falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga scores against Gerrard's boys
