Almanaku: Geiger's meeter inventor
And now a page from our almanach of "Sunday Morning: 30 September 1882, Both today 136 years ago and ONWARD... the day when future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the production of what is known as the Geiger gauge.
“A mainstary of science—since, Geiger’s meater became a mainstary of popular culture as ’150’’s’ Bell, with a cast of actors’ seemingly non-professional scientists such as Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger gauge used to track radioactive minerals, like uranium.
"When you put these headphones, you can actually hear the effects of atoms that are released from radioactivity to minerals."
Evans: "Don’t tell me, I'm really listening now!'
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name continues to live.
“A new anti-cancer vaccine can teach the immune system “distinguish” bad cells
“A new anti-cancer vaccine can teach the immune system “distinguished” bad cells and killing them
Vaccines teach the immune system to recognize bad cells as part of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient, changing them in the laboratory
They can �‘distinguish’ a protein commonly found in many types of cancer and then re-enlightened
A vaccine that is on the test is showing promising results in patients who have certain types of cancer.
A woman who was treated with the vaccine that teaches the immune system to recognize bad cells observed that ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient, changing them in the laboratory so that they ‘distinguish’ a protein commonly found in several types of cancer called HER2, and then in reinjecting these cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky from the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our results tell us that we're dealing with an incredibly promising vaccine."
Her2 "promotes the growth of several types of cancer," including breast, ovary, lung and colon cancers, Professor Berzofsky explained.
“A similar immune cell extraction’ approach by patients “to teach” them about how to detect cancer cells, has functioned in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
