HIV/Sida: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China has said there is a 14% increase in the number of people living with HIV and AIDS.
There are more than 8,200 people affected across the country, health authorities say.
In the second quarter of 2018 alone, about 40,000 new cases were reported.
Most of the new cases have been transmitted through sexual intercourse, displaying a change from the past.
Traditionally, the HIV virus spread rapidly to parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
But the number of people who contract the HIV virus in this way had almost gone zero, Chinese health officials said at a briefing in Yunnan province.
However, year after year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100,000 people.
The transmission of the HIV virus through sex is an acute issue in the LGBT community in China.
Homosexuality was de-criminalized in China in 1997, but it is said that discrimination against LGBT people still prevails.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have measured that 70-90% of men who have sex with other men will eventually marry women.
Much of the transmission of the disease occurs due to inappropriate protective measures during sex in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to medicines for the treatment of HIV as part of an attempted crackdown on this problem.
Maxine Waters denies that GOP senators’ records have been propagated by staff members, says these are �‘dangerous lies’ and ‘conspiracy theories’ ’
U.S. Representative Maxine Waters on Saturday denounced the allegations that a member of her staff had posted personal information of three U.S. Republican senators on the pages of lawmakers on Wikipedia.
The Los Angeles Democrat claimed that these claims were being pushed by experts and "far-right" websites.
"Lies, lies, and only disgustisfied lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
It is reported that the leaked information includes home addresses and phone numbers of U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both of Utah.
The information appeared online on Thursday, posted by an unknown person on Capitol Hill during a Senate panel's hearing about the misjudgment of a sexual nature against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
The leak came shortly after all three senators had questioned Kavanaugh.
Some of the conservative sites such as ‘Gateway Pundit’ and ‘RedState’, reported that IP addresses that identify the source of the publications were related to Waters’s office and extracted the data of a Waters staff member, Hill reports.
"This baseless claim is totally untrue and a big lie," Waters continued.
“Member of my staff — whose identity, personal information and security has been compromised as a result of these false and fabricated claims - has in no way been responsible for leaking this information.
"This baseless claim is totally false and a big lie."
Waters' statement was immediately confronted with criticism online, including former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"This disclaimer is done in a frustrating way," Fleischer wrote.
“This means she doesn’t have the right temper to be a Member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they didn’t do, he shouldn’’t get nervous.
It doesn't have to be a provocateur.
It shouldn't call into question the motives of the accuser.
He must be calm and peaceful."
Fleischer appeared to be compalising Waters' reaction to Democrats' criticism of referee Kavanaugh, who was accused by critics have appeared extremely nervous during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republican candidate who is running to take Waters in the mid-term elections, also posted his statements on Twitter.
"Big case, if it's going to be true," he tweeted.
In its statement Waters said that its office had signaled "the relevant authorities and law enforcement institutions on these false accusations.
"We're going to make sure that the perpetrators are revealed," she continued, "and that they assume legal responsibility for all their actions, which have a destructive and dangerous effect on anyone from my staff."
