What is the "Global Citizen Festival" and has done anything to reduce poverty?
“This Saturday, New York will host the “Global Citizen” Festival, an annual music show which contains a large and impressive list of stars who perform and also have an equally impressive rision; to end poverty in the world.
Now, in its seventh year, the ‘‘Global Citizen” Festival will host crowds of thousands of people in the ‘Great Law’ in Central Park to not only enjoy the interpretations of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to become aware of the show’’’’’’’’s extreme poverty by 2030.
““Global Citizen” festival, which started in 2012, is an extension of the “Global Poverty Project”, an international advocacy group, which hopes to end poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting against it.
In order to obtain a free-to-show ticket (except when you want to pay for a VIP ticket), participants have to fulfill a variety of tasks, or “activities” such as voluntary work, sending an email to a world leader, performing a phone call or any other meaningful way that would help raise awareness of achieving their goal of ending poverty.
But how successful was the "Global Citizen Festival" when there are 12 years left to achieve its goal?
Does the idea of rewarding people with a free concert a real way to convince them to look for these interventions, or is it just another case of what is known as “clipting activism” — people who feel they’re making a real change by signing an online petition or sending a message on Twitter?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has reportedly recorded more than 19 million "actions" from its supporters, giving a boost to different goals.
These actions are said to have helped to urge world leaders to declare $37 billion commitments and policies, which are said to affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 pledges and declarations as a result of the group's actions, where at least $10 billion has already been distributed or have been secured as funds.
The group estimates that the funds provided so far have yielded a direct impact to about 649 million people worldwide.
Some of the key pledges include “The Power of Nutrition””, a UK-based investors and implementing agencies committed to “help children grow up with their full potential,” promising to give Rwanda $35 million to help end malnutrition in this country, after receiving more than 4700 “tweets” from “Global Citize” activists.
"With the support of the British government, donors, national governments and citizens of the world like you, we can turn social injustice of malnutrition into a memory of the past," The Power ofnumination told the crowd at a concert directly in London in April 2018.
The group also said that after taking 5000 actions to call on the UK to improve nutrition for mothers and children, the government announced the financing of a project, “The Power of Nutrition”, which will affect 5 million women and children in need of food intervention.
In an answer to the most frequently asked questions on their website asking “What makes you believe that we can end extreme poverty?”
Global Citizen replied: "It's going to be a long and difficult journey - sometimes we're going to fall and we're going to fail.
But like any high civic right and as anti-apartheid movements ahead of us, we will succeed, because together we are more powerful.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of the actors who will perform at this year’’’s New York show, which will be presented by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jakman.
