National Trust observes the secret life of bats
A new research being conducted by a company in Scotland's mountainous areas is meaning to find out how bats use the terrain to hunt for food.
It is hoped that the new findings will shed new light on the behavior of unique flying mammalians and help defensive activities in the future.
The study by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow ordinary bats and those called soprano pipistrelles as well as long-eyed brown and Daubenton bats in Inverewe gardens, in Wester Ross.
Special registration devices will be placed at key points throughout the area to track the activities of bats throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct field studies using handheld detectors.
Analysis of the sounds to be carried out by experts for all recordings will determine the frequency of bat sounds and what each species does.
Subsequently, a report and a map of their habitat will be drawn to give a detailed picture of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature protection adviser for NTS, hopes conclusions will display which habitats are most important for bats and how they are used by each species.
This information would help determine the benefits of the administration of settlements, such as the creation of pastures, and how to maintain forests for bats and other related species.
The number of bats in Scotland and across the UK has dropped sharply over the last century.
They are under threat of construction and development works that have an impact on nests and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as adhesive letters or the chemical components of building materials, as well as pet attacks.
The bats aren't blind.
However, due to their hunting habits at night, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to grappling prey.
They use a sophisticated location finding technique through sounds to accurately define insects and in-flight obstacles.
NTS, which is responsible for the care of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land in the country, deals very seriously with bats.
There are ten trained experts, who regularly conduct studies, socket inspections and are sometimes involved in bailout programs.
The organisation has even set up the first and only reserve in Scotland dedicated to bats in Threave, Dumfries and Galloway, which serves as home for eight out of ten bat species in Scotland.
The manager of this reserve, David Thompson, says that this country is the ideal territory for them.
"Here on Threave we have an excellent area for bates," he said.
“We have old buildings, lots of ancient trees and all the right habitat.”
But there’s still a lot about bats that remain unknown, so the work we do here and elsewhere will help us figure out more what they need to ‘develop.’”
He emphasizes the importance of carrying out-nat-night checks before performing property maintenance works because there is a possibility that the unintended destruction of a nest can kill up to 400 female and young nabs by exterminating the entire population of an area.
Nairs are protected and their murder, harassment and distress or the destruction of their nests is outlaws.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish clerk at the Trust for the Conservation of Nait Natures, encourages people to become part of help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and for many of our species, we just don’t know how their population is shifting."
