The U.N. "will regret forever" if it loses the status of the woble's leader in post-Brexit car production, business secretary Greg Clark said.
He added it was "concerning" that Toyota in the UK had told the BBC that if Britain left the EU without a deal, society would temporarily halt production at its facility in Burnaston, near Derby.
"We need an agreement," Mr. Clark said.
The Japanese carmaker said the impact of delays at the border, in the case of a no-deal exit, would cost the loss of jobs.
The factory in Burnaston - which manufactures Toyota Auris and Avensis models - produced almost 150 000 cars last year, of which 90% were exported to other European Union countries.
"In my judgment, in the event that Britain leaves the EU at the end of March, we're going to stop production at our plant," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's administrative director at Burnaston.
Other carmakers in the UK, such as Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover have expressed fears of leaving the EU without a deal and how cross-cross trade will work.
For example, BMW, said that after Brexit it will close for a month its Mini series factory.
The main concerns relate to what car manufacturers refer to as supply chain risks in the case of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota��’s production line operates on “immediately once ordered,” with parts coming every 37 minutes from suppliers from the UK and the EU, for custom cars.
If, on March 29, the UK leaves the EU without an agreement, there could be delays at the limit, which according to this industry could lead to delays and lack of parts.
It would be impossible for Toyota to keep at its Derbyshire in inventory of more than a day, the company said, and therefore production would be halted.
Clark said Theresa May’s ‘Chequers’ plan for future relations with the EU “is specially built to avoid these border controls.”
“We need an agreement. We want to have the best agreement that will allow us not only to enjoy the success of the moment for also to seize this opportunity,” he told Today’s ‘Radio 4’ programme.’
“The data, not only from Toyota but also from other manufacturers, shows that we obviously have to be able to continue what has been a fairly successful supply chain.’”
Toyota couldn't determine how long it's going to stop production, but in the long term, warned that added costs would lower the factory's competitiveness and consequently lead to job reductions.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working in Burnaston for 24 years and who is the senior official of the Unite union at the factory, said members are becoming increasingly concerned: "According to my experience these jobs are closed, they are no longer returned.
A government spokesman said: "We have set a punctual and credible plan for our relationship with the EU in the future."
