Severies may contradict this statement: Sanders hasn't held a press conference at the White House since September 10.
Moderator Chris Wallace raised the question why.
Sanders said the lack of press conferences didn't come as a result of dissatisfaction with the attitude of TV journalists, although she said: "I won't deny the fact that they attract attention."
Afterwards, she hinted that direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
"The president conducts more sessions of guaranteeing the quality of any other president ahead of him," she said, adding, without quoting, "We've seen these figures."
“It’s going to continue to have press conferences,” Sanders said, “whether the press has the opportunity to ask questions directly to the president of the United States, then that’s much better than talking to me.
"We're trying to do that often and you've seen that we've done it frequently over the last few weeks, replacing press conferences when you can talk to the President of the United States."
Trump regularly accepts questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in open sessions, or at press conferences with important personalities.
Individual press conferences are rare.
The president probably indicated the reason for this fact, during this week, in New York, where he made a controversial and in some strange cases, in front of journalists.
Health secretary writes to EU employees, on NHS Scotland, about fears over Brexit
The health secretary has written to EU staff working at the NHS in Scotland, to express his gratitude on behalf of the country and the desire to stay even after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, has sent a less than six months away from the UK.
The Scottish government has already undertaken to cover the costs of applying for permanent resident status for EU citizens working in public services locally.
In her letter, Ms Freeman wrote: "During the summer, negotiations have continued on the departure of the UK from the EU, approachinging a decision in the autumn.
But, the UK government has also intensified its preparations in the case of a no-deal scenario.
I know it's a pretty disturbing time for you all.
For this reason, I wanted to emphasize how much I appreciate the contribution of every member of staff, regardless of their nationality.
Colleagues from the EU and beyond, bring valuable experience and skills that enhance and improve the work of the health service, by increasing the benefits of the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home and we very much want you to stay here."
Christion Abercrombie undergoes emergency surgery after head injury
Tennessee State centre-bast protector Christion Abercrombie underwent immediate surgery after a head injury suffered on Saturday in a 31-27 loss against Vanderbilt Commodore, The Tennessian journalist Mike Organ reported.
Tennessee State coach Rod Reed told reporters that the injury occurred shortly before the first.
"He came to the side and collapsed there," Reed said.
Trainers and medical personnel gave Abercrombie oxygen on the side line before placing him on the stretcher and sending it for further examinations.
A Tennessee State official told WSMV’’’’s “HRid Harris” in Našville that Abercrombie had completed surgery at the Vanderbilt Hospital.
Harris added that "there is still no details on the type/scale of injury" and Tennessee State is trying to figure out when the damage occurred.
Abercrombie, a second-year student, is in his first season with Tennessee State after being transferred from Illinois.
He made five tackles on Saturday before leaving the game, bringing the total number of tackles during the season to 18.
Buyers with foreign nationalities will pay higher stamp taxes when buying a property in the UK
Buyers with foreign nationalities will pay higher stamp taxes for the purchase of a property in the UK and the extra money will be used to help the homeless according to new Conservative Party (Tory) plans
This move will neutralize Corbyn’’s initiative’s success to attract young voters
Stamp tax will be applied to those who don’t pay taxes in the UK
Treasury department envisages to raise up to 120 million GBP per year to help the homeless
Theresa May will announce today that buyers of foreign nationalities will pay higher stamp taxes for the purchase of a property in the UK and additional money will be used to help the homeless.
This move will be seen as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn’s initiative’s success to attract young voters with promises to offer more affordable housing and “target“ high-earning persons.
The stamp tax will apply to individuals and firms that do not pay taxes in the UK, while the additional money will support the government's initiative to combat a lack of housing.
This extra tariff — which is added to the current stamp tax, including an increase in the tax rate introduced two years ago for second-hoses and for properties purchased to be leased — can go up to three percent.
The Treasury Department predicts that this move will accumulate up to 120 million GBP per year.
About 13 per cent of properties recently built in London are bought by people residing outside the UK, which causes prices to rise and makes it more difficult to find housing by buyers for the first time.
Many rich areas of the country — particularly in the capital — have become "phostom cities" due to the high number of buyers of foreign nationalities who spend most of their time abroad.
This new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction of the stamp tax to help young people buy their first home.
He accused large construction firms of holding high prices by buying land but without putting into use, and urged Ms. May to give up quotas for affordable homes, with the aim of adjusting Britain's "shame with properties."
Mr Corbyn has proposed some attractive housing reforms, including lease control and the removal of "withous" departures.
Also, he wants to give more power to the advice to build new houses.
Mrs May said: "Last year I stated that I would dedicate the mandate of the prime minister to the return of the British dream - for a better life for any new generation.
And that's how it's to regulate the problematic housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build a life here.
However, it is not right that individuals, who do not live in the UK, and foreign companies find it easier to buy homes than British workers.
“For many, the dream of owning a home has become something incredibly difficult to achieve, and the injustice against the homeless is more than real.”
Jack Ross: 'My biggest ambition is to train Scotland'
Sunderland coach Jack Ross say his "greatest ambituity" is that at a certain stage he will become Scotland's coach.
The Scot, 42, is enjoying the challenge of rebuilding the north-eastern club, which is currently positioned in third place in League One (third category), three points away from the top.
He arrived this summer at the Light Stadium after returning St Mirren to the Scottish Premier League last season.
“I wanted to play for my place as a player.
"I showed up once at the B representative and that's all," Ross told BBC Scotland Sportsound.
“But as a child I grew up seeing Scotland in Hampden with my father, and it’s something that has always attracted me.
However, this opportunity will only come if I'm successful in running clubs."
Ross' predecessors in running Sunderland include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O’Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic coach says he's not worried he's following in the footsteps of these big names at such a big club, having previously rejected offers from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"At the moment, my success will depend on whether 'I can turn this team into the Premier League?'
Given the structure and facilities of this club, it definitely has its place in the Premier League," he said.
"It's not an easy task to go there, but I'd only see myself as successful if I managed to bring the club back to the Premier League.
Ross has only spent three years starting his coaching career after as an auxiliary-coach in Dumbarton and a 15-mont-mont-month period on Hearts management staff.
Then he helped Alloá climb a category higher to the third round of the cup, as well as transformed St Mirren from a club close to relegation from the category to next season's Championship title winners.
And now, Ross says he feels better than he's ever felt during his career as a player at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
“Maybe it was really a turning point,” he says, for taking the helm of Alloa.
“I really believed that training was the right thing for me, rather than being a player.
It seems strange because I played well, I had a good life thanks to this profession, and I enjoyed some good achievements.
But being a player can be difficult.
You've got a lot of things, every week.
Again, I experience a lot of stress and pressure from work but the training seems the right job.
"I've always wanted to train and now I'm doing something like this. I've never been so good and so myself all my life as an adult."
You can hear the full interview on Sunday, in Sportsound on September 30, on Radio Scotland from 12:00 to 13:00 BST
According to a study, the perfect time "to drink a beer" is on Saturday at 5:30 p.m.
Wine heatwave has boosted the proceeds of bars in a difficult situation in Britain, but has increased pressure on restaurant chains.
The data shows that sales in groups of premises and bars increased by 2.7 percent in July - but purchases in restaurants fell by 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin, business consultant at CGA, who compiled the data, said: "Inconsistent sunny weather as well as England’s participation in the World Cup longer than expected influenced July to follow the same trend as June, where bar sales rose by 2.8 per cent, while restaurants recorded even higher declines.
The lap in restaurants fell by 1.8% in June, and deteriorated even further in July.
Bars and premises with main activity providing beverages recorded the best performance, with growth higher than the reduction of trade in restaurants.
The premises with the main activity the provision of food also recognized the decline as a result of sunny weather, although not as significant as restaurants.
It seems that people just want to go out for a drink.
Liquor sales in bars and bars increased by 6.6 percent that month, while food fell by three percent."
Paul Newman, free-time and hospitality analyst at RSM said: "These results confirm the trend we've seen since the end of April.
When it comes to sales in the non-home market, the weather and the impact of important social or sporting events remain the main factors.
It's no wonder that restaurant groups continue to encounter difficulties, although a decrease in sales by 4.8 percent a year will have a particularly heavy basquement considering the constant pressure of costs.
"The long and hot summer couldn't come at a more difficult moment for food operators, and time will tell if August's more average temperatures will give the long-desired composure."
Total salary growth in bars and restaurants, including newly opened ones, was 2.7 percent in July, reflecting the slowdown in brand launch.
The Coffer Peach index of industry sales tracing, for the UK's bar and restaurant sector, collects and analyzes data from 47 companies, with total turnover of more than GBP 9 billion, and is the industry's benchmark.
