Bay pump advertisement that compares mothers with cows divides public opinion
A society for the production of breast pumps has divided the public opinion with an ad that compares mothers who breastfeed with cows milking.
To begin promoting what is considered the "world's "noisisable and unraveling breast pump," the company of consumer technology products, Elvie, displayed a hilarious music video, to advertise the freedom that this new pump gives to mothers who need to express themselves freely.
Four real mothers dance in a bag-filled cow stall under the sound of a song with lyrics as follows: "Yes, I milk myself, but I don’t tail" and “If you didn’t notice, these aren’t sies, it’s my chest.””
Refrain continues: "Pompoje, I'm feeding babies, pompo, pompoja, you're milking ladies."
However, the advertisement, which has been published on the firm's Facebook page, has sparked online debates.
With 77 000 views and hundreds of comments, the video has received mixed reactions from viewers, with many expressing that it underestimates the "horrors" of the dairy industry.
“Using cows to advertise this product is a very poor decision.
Just as us, they must remain "pregnantic" and "give" to produce milk, only that their "babies" are taken from them a few days after they are born," someone wrote.
Elvie breast pump is placed within bras and does not stand out (Elvie/Mother)
Another commented: “Clearly traumatic for the mother and for the child.
Why don’t we use to advertise a breast pump for mothers who can keep their babies?”
Someone else added: "What an advertisement without empathy."
Others supported the advertisement, among which a woman admitted that the song seemed "to laugh."
"I think that's a genius idea.
I'd have bought one if you'd still breastfeed.
Pumping caused me to feel exactly like a cow.
Advertising is a little crazy but I saw what it implies.
This is a genius product," someone wrote.
Another commented: "This is a hilarious advertisement addressed to mothers who use the pump (often in the workplace or in the toilet) and who feel like "cows."
This isn't an advertisement that evaluates or judges the dairy industry."
At the end of the video, the women’s group shows that they have all danced with the "secret" pump placed in bras.
The concept behind this campaign is based on the data that many women who use breast pump say they feel the cows.
However, the Elvie pump is noise-free, there are no threads or pipes, is placed inside the bras and does not stand out, giving women freedom to move, keeping their babies, and even going outside while using it.
Ana Balarin, partner and ECD (Creative Executive Director) in Mother commented: “Elvis pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a bold and provocative promotion.
Drawing a parallel between women who want to express themselves freely and dairy cows, we wanted to note the use of the breast pump and the challenges encountered, while also displaying in a fun and similar way the extraordinary feeling of freedom that the new pump gives.
This isn’t the first time the Elvie pump comes out on the front page.
During London fashion week, a mother-of-two appeared on the catwalk of designer Marta Jakubowski using the product.
