That's where we need to start.
Talk to your spouse and your children: You can't prepare your family to take care of you if you don’t tell your wishes first.
Work with the advisor and with your family and discuss where and how to take care, as these choices can be an important factor in determining costs.
Get your financial advisor too: The advisor can help you to find a way to pay expenses.
Options for financing long-term care include a traditional insurance policy for long-term care, hybrid life insurance policies to help cover these expenses or self-insurance through your wealth - as long as you have money.
Prepare legal documents: Avoid legal battles through an authorization.
Take a health care proxy and assign a trusted person who will supervise your health care and guarantee that experts act in accordance with your wishes in case you are unable to communicate.
Also, consider taking a proxy for your finances.
Choose a trusted person who will make financial decisions on your behalf and will guarantee the payment of invoices if you can't do something like this yourself.
