Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have died as a result of a powerful earthquake and the tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, officials have said as the death toll is expected to rise.
With out-of-the-function communications networks, assistance workers have failed to receive information from the Donggala regency, an area north of Palu, which was closest to the epicentre of the 7.5-magnitude Earthquake.
In Palu, more than 16 000 people have been evacuated after this disaster.
Below are some facts about Palu and Donggala, on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of the central Sulawesi province, located at the bottom of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, with a population of nearly 379 800, in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when it was hit by the earthquake and tsunami.
Donggala is a regency that extends more than 300 km (180 miles) coastline length northwest of Sulawesi Island.
The regency, an administrative region smaller than the province, had an estimated population of about 299 200 inhabitants in 2017.
Fishing and livestock are the province's main economic activities, especially in the Dongala coastal region.
Excretion of nické is also important in the province, but it is mainly concentrated in Morowali, on the other side of Sulawesi's coast.
According to the Disastrection Agency in Indonesia, Palu and Donggala have been hit several times by the tsunami over the past 100 years.
In 1938, a tsunami claimed the lives of more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of homes in Dongala.
In 1996, another tsunami struck the western part of Donggala, causing nine casualties.
Indonesia is located in the seismic area of the Ring of Fire in the Pacific Ocean and is regularly hit by earthquakes.
Below you will find some of the biggest earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
On December 26, a powerful earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province, in northern Sumatra, triggered a tsunami that hit 14 states, causing 226 000 casualties along the coastline of the Indian Ocean, of which more than half lived in Aceh.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds were killed on Nias Island on the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude Earthquake struck south of Java, Indonesia’’’’’’’’’’’’’’’’’s most populous island, triggering a tsunami crashing into the south coast and killing about 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude earthquake near the city of Padang, the capital of western Sumatra province.
More than 1100 people died.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake struck one of Mentawai's islands in Sumatra, triggering a tsunami of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow earthquake hit Pidie Jaya regency in Aceh, bringing destruction and panicking to people after reminding them of destruction caused by the deadly earthquake and tsunami of 2004.
This time, there were no tsunamis, but more than 100 people lost their lives from building collapse.
2018: Powerful earthquakes hit the tourist island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, most of them in the northern part of the island.
The earthquake destroyed thousands of buildings and temporarily blocked thousands of tourists.
