Federal prosecutors seek rare death sentence for NYC terror attack suspect
Federal prosecutors in New York but demand the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the suspect of the terrorist attack in New York City that killed eight people -- a rare sentence that has not been imposed for a federal crime since 1953.
In October, Saipov, 30-year-old, supposedly used a Home Depot rental truck to carry out the terrorist attack on a bicycle route along the western highway, downstaisth, trampling pedestrians and cyclists.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov "premeditatedly" killed the eight victims and caused serious "premeditated" bodily injuries, according to the letter of seeking the death penalty, set out in New York's southern county.
According to a court document, both of these charges can be given the death penalty.
Weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury put Saipov in front of 22 indictments, including eight counts of homicide in organized crime, charges often used by prosecutors in organised crime cases, and one counts of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
This attack came as a result of "significant and premeditated planning," prosecutors said, describing Saipov's way of executing the crime as "disgusting, cruel and moralless."
“Sayfullo Habibullaević Saipov inflicted injuries, injuries, injuries and losses to the families of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza, and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco.”
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's been a decade since the last time New York's southern county prosecuted a death penalty.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was convicted of the murder of two drug suppliers but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The last when the death penalty was used in a federal case in New York was in 1953 for Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple who were executed after being convicted of having committed conspiracy and spying for the benefit of the Soviet Union two years before the Cold War.
Both were executed in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to the court documents, Saipov, with Uzbek nationalities, showed no remorse during the days and months after the attack.
He has told investigators that he feels good about what he has done, the police announced.
According to the indictment, Saipov told authorities that he was inspired to carry out the attack after seeing ISIS videos on his cellphone.
Police stated that he had also asked to place the ISIS flag in his hospital room.
He has pleaded not guilty to 22 lawsuits.
David Patton, one of the public lawyers representing Saipov, said they are "very disappointed with the prosecution's decision.
“We kneel that the decision to seek the death penalty and not admission of guilt coupled with life imprisonment without the possibility of appeal will further increase the trauma caused caused by these events to all the persons involved,” Patton said.
Saipov's defence team had previously asked prosecutors not to seek the death penalty.
