The number of defectors in North Korea decreases under the leadership of Kim
The number of defectors in North Korea has fallen since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, a South Korean lawmaker has said.
Park Byeong-seug, quoting data from South Korea's Union Ministry, has stated that there were about 1127 defections last year - compared to 2706 in 2011.
Mr. Park said the main factors were the strictest controls on the borders between North Korea and China as well as the highest fees demanded by human traffickers.
Pyongyang has made no public comments.
The majority of North Korean defectors receive citizenship from South Korea.
Seoul says more than 30 000 people from North Korea have crossed the border illegally since the end of Korea's war in 1953.
Most leave for China, which has the highest littitude of the border with North Korea, and it can be more simply passed compared to the Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas, which is protected by multiple forces.
China considers defectors illegal immigrants and not as refugees and often repatriates them by force.
Relations between North and South Korea — which are technically still at war — have improved sharply in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang and their talks focused on long-delayed denuclearization negotiations.
This meeting came after the historic June meeting between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, where they agreed, in general terms, to work on a nuclear-free Korean peninsula.
But on Saturday, North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho blamed U.S. sanctions for the lack of progress so far.
“Without having confidence in the U.S. “Without there will be confidence in our national security, and in these circumstances, there is no chance that we unilaterally disarmament first,” said Mr. Ri during a speech at the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
